The general duties of the city clerk are to:
(1) Keep correct and full minutes of the proceedings of city commission together with all ordinances and resolutions passed by it, in a book or register kept for that purpose;
(2) Receive all applications or petitions made to the city and place them before the city manager at the meeting of the board of commissioners next succeeding the receipt thereof;
(3) See that a docket is kept wherein shall be entered the names of all persons tried and fined by the municipal court, the nature of the offense, date of trial, amount of fine and return of the police officer thereon;
(4) Attend all commission meetings;
(5) Receive all applications for licenses and to issue the same as prescribed by ordinance;
(6) Carefully preserve the records of the city and all valuable documents placed in his hands, as clerk, and while such records and documents are free and open to inspection, not to permit them to be removed or taken away except by order of the board of commissioners;
(7) Keep a correct digest of all returns of taxable property in the city;
(8) Issue all orders, summons, executions, notices or other instruments which may be required of him;
(9) Keep the following books:
a. A book of rough sheet of minutes; a book of neat and accurate minutes;
b. A book for recording licenses issued;
c. An ordinance book;
d. A book of minutes and a docket of the municipal court;
e. A book of charges in which all costs must be entered as coming into his hands, from whom, when, what account and the amount;
f. An account of all moneys paid into the city treasury;
g. A checkbook which shall contain a margin showing the number, amount, date, for what purpose ordered, and to whom issued in each case;
h. A book in which he shall register the names and places of business, and date of license of each person doing or carrying on any business, calling, trade or profession in the city, under the proper classification.
(Code 1969, § 2-29)

